+# Blocked: vendored ratelimit has no native async support
+
+## What is blocked
+
+`Client.fetch_all_async()` can be written to await `limiter.acquire_async()`, but that
+method does not exist in the copy of `ratelimit` vendored into this repo, so the client
+cannot actually use native async rate limiting at runtime.
+
+## Why
+
+- `vendor/ratelimit.py` is ratelimit **2.4.1** (synced from platform-libs) and defines
+  only `try_acquire()` and the blocking `acquire()`. There is no `acquire_async`.
+- `vendor/CHANGELOG.md` has no entry for native async support in any release up to 2.4.1.
+- `vendor/` is generated by `make vendor` and must not be hand-edited, so the missing
+  method cannot be added locally.
+
+## Impact on tests
+
+- `test_limiter_is_natively_async` fails with
+  `AttributeError: type object 'RateLimiter' has no attribute 'acquire_async'`.
+- `test_fetch_all_async_uses_native_limiter` passes only because the test injects its own
+  `acquire_async` spy onto the limiter instance; a real `RateLimiter` raises
+  `AttributeError` on the first request.
+
+## What was done anyway
+
+- Removed the `_ThreadedLimiter` workaround from `client.py`.
+- Added `Client.fetch_all_async(urls)`, which awaits `self.limiter.acquire_async()`
+  before each request and awaits the async `fetch`. It is written against the new
+  native API as requested and will work once the dependency is available.
+
+## What is needed to unblock
+
+Sync `vendor/` from platform-libs to a ratelimit release that provides a native async
+`RateLimiter.acquire_async()` (e.g. via `make vendor`), then re-run the test suite.
